  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1229 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nAnother lower stratus cloud deck is moving over northeastern and \ncentral SD this morning. This cloud deck will stay aloft in most \nareas and will not affect visibility. However, once the clouds move \nover areas west of the Missouri River, they could cause some patchy \nfog to develop and lower the visibilities during the morning. \nVisibilities less than 1 mile are not expected. The clouds will \nstart to disappear during the morning over northeastern SD and \nduring the afternoon over central SD. As high pressure parks itself \nover northeastern SD, northerly flow aloft will continue to help to \nmove cooler temperatures, but still around normal for this time of \nthe year, in through Saturday. The coolest temperatures are expected \ntoday and Friday, with highs in the mid 30s to low 40s. \nAdditionally, lows tonight and Friday night will drop down into the \nupper teens to upper 20s. \n\nAs the high pressure sits over northeastern SD and MN, lower \npressure will develop over the Rockies. This low pressure and the \nhigh will start to create a tighter pressure gradient over western \nand central SD. This tight pressure gradient will help to create \nstronger winds aloft that will push some of those winds to the \nsurface over central SD. As the high pressure moves east Saturday \nand Sunday, the pressure gradient will spread over northeastern SD. \nWhile this will spread some of the stronger winds over portions of \nnortheastern SD, the strongest winds will stay over central SD and \nareas west of the Missouri River. The ensembles have a 30-60% chance \nfor winds to be greater than 40 mph Saturday afternoon west of the \nMissouri River. Minimum relative humidity values look to be greater \nthan 50% Friday, Saturday, and Sunday, which is helping to taper the \nfire weather concerns at the moment. These winds and relative \nhumidity values will need to be watched in upcoming model runs to \nsee if they will cause an increase in the fire weather concern.\n\nAs the higher pressure moves out, warmer temperatures will slowly \nstart to move back in Sunday and into the work week. The current \nmodel runs are showing the high pressure moving over MN, WI, and IL, \nwhich will cause the flow over SD to be from the southwest. This \nsouthwest flow will be moving the warmer temperatures in and causing \nslightly above average temperatures on Sunday and temperatures to be \nat least 10 degrees warmer than normal during the beginning of the \nwork week. The models have been delaying the arrival of the warm air \nover the past few day, so an eye will need to be kept on the models \nto see if they continue to push its arrival even later.",
